# USB through the kernel's Android gadget (docs/design/usb-mtp.md): MTP and PTP,
# adb through FunctionFS inside the gadget. The modules are loaded and checked at
# post-fs (/vendor/etc/init/init.pw3.usb.modules.rc). The functions follow
# sys.usb.config, set from persist.sys.usb.config at boot and then by
# UsbDeviceManager; the handlers are AOSP's init.usb.rc ones for a non-configfs
# gadget (init.pw3-main.rc does not import that file), except that the ones
# carrying adb are in two steps -- see the comment above them. adb needs
# authentication (ro.adb.secure=1) and has no TCP transport (adbd, system/core
# 0002).
#
# What persist.sys.usb.config holds is not this port's choice: UsbDeviceManager
# strips mtp and ptp out of it at every boot ("Previous versions can set persist
# config to mtp/ptp but it does not get reset on OTA", UsbDeviceManager.java),
# and getDefaultFunctions() answers "adb" alone once adb is enabled. So a device
# that has been through one framework start comes up as the adb gadget, and MTP
# is what UsbDeviceManager switches to when a computer is on the cable and the
# reader allows access to the data. The product default below it (mtp) is only
# what the very first boot sees.
on init
    setprop sys.usb.configfs 0
    setprop sys.usb.ffs.ready 0
    setprop sys.pw3.ffs_ready 0
    setprop sys.usb.state none
    setprop service.adb.tcp.port -1
    mkdir /dev/usb-ffs 0770 shell shell
    mkdir /dev/usb-ffs/adb 0770 shell shell

on post-fs-data
    mkdir /data/misc/adb 02750 system shell
    mkdir /data/adb 0700 root root
    restorecon_recursive /data/misc/adb

# Once the gadget is verified and persistent properties are loaded: the identity
# strings, the per-device serial (pw3-usb-check serial), adb's FunctionFS.
on boot && property:sys.pw3.usb.phase=gadget-bound
    exec_start pw3-usb-serial
    write /sys/class/android_usb/android0/iManufacturer ${ro.product.manufacturer}
    write /sys/class/android_usb/android0/iProduct ${ro.product.model}
    write /sys/class/android_usb/android0/iSerial ${persist.pw3.usb.serial}
    write /sys/class/android_usb/android0/f_ffs/aliases adb
    # Old FunctionFS defaults its root to 0500. The read-only checker has
    # supplementary shell membership; endpoint files remain owner-only 0600.
    mount functionfs adb /dev/usb-ffs/adb uid=2000,gid=2000,rmode=0550
    exec_start pw3-ffs-check
    setprop sys.usb.config ${persist.sys.usb.config}

on property:sys.usb.config=none && property:sys.usb.configfs=0
    stop adbd
    write /sys/class/android_usb/android0/enable 0
    write /sys/class/android_usb/android0/bDeviceClass 0
    setprop sys.usb.state ${sys.usb.config}

# Vendor 18d1 with the product IDs Android uses for these functions (AOSP
# init.usb.rc, Nexus devices): computers then know the device class at once.
#
# Every configuration with adb in it is enabled in two steps, because the
# descriptors of adb's FunctionFS are adbd's to write and the gadget cannot bind
# the function before they are there: adbd writes them and then sets
# sys.usb.ffs.ready (system/core/adb/daemon/usb.cpp), which is what every AOSP
# device routing adb through FunctionFS waits for (init.usb.configfs.rc,
# device/linaro/hikey/init.common.usb.rc). AOSP's one-step handler, which this
# file used to copy, is for the gadget's own adb function, which needs nothing
# from user space. Enabling first and starting adbd after left the cable dead on
# a boot that asked for adb -- with it, MTP too, there being one gadget ("插线了
# 啊", with nothing on the bus, user, 2026-10-20).
#
# sys.pw3.ffs_ready is this port's own gate and stays where it belongs: adbd does
# not start at all unless the FunctionFS mount is the real thing and adb
# authentication is on (pw3-init-check usb).
on property:sys.usb.config=adb && property:sys.usb.configfs=0 && property:sys.pw3.ffs_ready=1
    start adbd

on property:sys.usb.ffs.ready=1 && property:sys.usb.config=adb && property:sys.usb.configfs=0
    write /sys/class/android_usb/android0/enable 0
    write /sys/class/android_usb/android0/idVendor 18d1
    write /sys/class/android_usb/android0/idProduct 4EE7
    write /sys/class/android_usb/android0/functions ${sys.usb.config}
    write /sys/class/android_usb/android0/enable 1
    setprop sys.usb.state ${sys.usb.config}

on property:sys.usb.config=mtp && property:sys.usb.configfs=0
    write /sys/class/android_usb/android0/enable 0
    write /sys/class/android_usb/android0/idVendor 18d1
    write /sys/class/android_usb/android0/idProduct 4EE1
    write /sys/class/android_usb/android0/functions ${sys.usb.config}
    write /sys/class/android_usb/android0/enable 1
    setprop sys.usb.state ${sys.usb.config}

on property:sys.usb.config=mtp,adb && property:sys.usb.configfs=0 && property:sys.pw3.ffs_ready=1
    start adbd

on property:sys.usb.ffs.ready=1 && property:sys.usb.config=mtp,adb && property:sys.usb.configfs=0
    write /sys/class/android_usb/android0/enable 0
    write /sys/class/android_usb/android0/idVendor 18d1
    write /sys/class/android_usb/android0/idProduct 4EE2
    write /sys/class/android_usb/android0/functions ${sys.usb.config}
    write /sys/class/android_usb/android0/enable 1
    setprop sys.usb.state ${sys.usb.config}

on property:sys.usb.config=ptp && property:sys.usb.configfs=0
    write /sys/class/android_usb/android0/enable 0
    write /sys/class/android_usb/android0/idVendor 18d1
    write /sys/class/android_usb/android0/idProduct 4EE5
    write /sys/class/android_usb/android0/functions ${sys.usb.config}
    write /sys/class/android_usb/android0/enable 1
    setprop sys.usb.state ${sys.usb.config}

on property:sys.usb.config=ptp,adb && property:sys.usb.configfs=0 && property:sys.pw3.ffs_ready=1
    start adbd

on property:sys.usb.ffs.ready=1 && property:sys.usb.config=ptp,adb && property:sys.usb.configfs=0
    write /sys/class/android_usb/android0/enable 0
    write /sys/class/android_usb/android0/idVendor 18d1
    write /sys/class/android_usb/android0/idProduct 4EE6
    write /sys/class/android_usb/android0/functions ${sys.usb.config}
    write /sys/class/android_usb/android0/enable 1
    setprop sys.usb.state ${sys.usb.config}

on property:init.svc.adbd=stopped
    setprop sys.usb.ffs.ready 0

# And the restart that is not a stop. `adb root` makes adbd exit so that init
# starts it again as root, and a service that exits without having been disabled
# or reset never reaches "stopped": init takes the restart branch and publishes
# "restarting" (system/core/init/service.cpp, Service::Reap). So the handler
# above does not fire and sys.usb.ffs.ready stays 1 while there is no adbd and no
# descriptors -- a property saying a thing that is not true, which is the whole
# of what this line is for. onrestart runs in Reap() before that state change,
# which is exactly where the truth is: adbd has gone, so its descriptors have
# gone with it. Nothing triggers on the 0, so no register is touched while there
# may be no cable (the V44 hazard).
#
# It is not a fix for anything, and the reason written here until 2026-10-21 was
# wrong. It said the new adbd's own `setprop sys.usb.ffs.ready 1` would be no
# change for init to trigger on, so the gadget would never be taken through
# enable 0 / enable 1 again. Init does not work that way: PropertySetImpl calls
# property_changed() after every successful update with no comparison to the old
# value, property_changed() queues it, and Action::CheckPropertyTriggers()
# matches the trigger's value against the new one and never looks at the old
# (system/core/init/property_service.cpp, init.cpp, action.cpp -- all three read
# in the tree on 2026-10-21). Setting 1 over 1 fires the section.
#
# What was actually observed: on 2026-10-21, after `adb root`, `adb devices` was
# empty for 80 seconds with the device still enumerated and Android still
# running. The explanation is not here: this machine runs two adb servers and
# the device moves between them (docs/known-issues.md section 3). What was
# measured on 2026-10-29 is the framework-restart case -- the device moved from
# port 5097 to 5038 while it stayed on the bus throughout; the `adb root` case
# was never checked against the other port at the time, so taking it for the
# same thing is Occam's razor, not a measurement. Whether a replug helped that
# day is a detail two records disagree on and nobody can check now, so this
# stops retelling it.
#
# Confirmed on 2026-10-29 (V93): one framework restart was watched for 100
# seconds, and the device's entry in `ioreg -p IOUSB` never went away while the
# device moved from adb server 5097 to 5038. Nothing here touches USB on a
# restart; an empty `adb devices` is the server being asked, not the gadget. See
# docs/known-issues.md section 3, which also records the wrong conclusion drawn
# earlier that day and the bad measurement behind it.
service adbd /system/bin/adbd --root_seclabel=u:r:su:s0
    class core
    socket adbd stream 660 system system
    disabled
    seclabel u:r:adbd:s0
    onrestart setprop sys.usb.ffs.ready 0
